613281},"charspan":[0,0],"page_no":1}],"references":[],"self_ref":"#/tables/0"}],"texts":[{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"4.6.4 Learners' computer literacy and utilisation of online electronic books","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":689.3333333333334,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":106.66666666666667,"r":492.0,"t":705.6666666666666},"charspan":[0,76],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/0","text":"4.6.4 Learners' computer literacy and utilisation of online electronic books"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"The study also explored the influence of learners' computer literacy on utilisation of online electronic books. In order to accomplish this task, respondents were asked to indicate the extent   to which learners computer literacy influenced utilisation of online electronic books. The results are summarised in Table 4.31.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":582.3333333333334,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":106.66666666666667,"r":542.6666666666666,"t":680.0},"charspan":[0,322],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/1","text":"The study also explored the influence of learners' computer literacy on utilisation of online electronic books. In order to accomplish this task, respondents were asked to indicate the extent   to which learners computer literacy influenced utilisation of online electronic books. The results are summarised in Table 4.31."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"caption","orig":"Table 4.31: Learners' computer literacy and utilisation of online electronic books","parent":{"$ref":"#/tables/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":538.6666666666666,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":106.66666666666667,"r":525.0,"t":553.6666666666666},"charspan":[0,82],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/2","text":"Table 4.31: Learners' computer literacy and utilisation of online electronic books"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"On the basis of data shown in Table 4.31, it emerges that 61 (23.6%) and 79 (30.4%) respondents scored in the Not at all and less extent level respectively. The information in Table 4.31 also reveal that 22 (10.6%) and 37 (14.3%) respondents scored in the extent and very great extent, respectively while 55(21.1%) said were not sure. The data gives the impression that majority 140 (54%) respondents did not support the view that learners' computer literacy influence utilisation of online electronic books compared to only, 59 (24.9%) respondents supporting the opinion: The implication of this finding is that   there was less   influence of learners= computer  literacy on   utilisation of online electronic books among the distance learning standards at the University of Nairobi. great they","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":89.0,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":105.33333333333333,"r":542.5517372590683,"t":326.6666666666667},"charspan":[0,796],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/3","text":"On the basis of data shown in Table 4.31, it emerges that 61 (23.6%) and 79 (30.4%) respondents scored in the Not at all and less extent level respectively. The information in Table 4.31 also reveal that 22 (10.6%) and 37 (14.3%) respondents scored in the extent and very great extent, respectively while 55(21.1%) said were not sure. The data gives the impression that majority 140 (54%) respondents did not support the view that learners' computer literacy influence utilisation of online electronic books compared to only, 59 (24.9%) respondents supporting the opinion: The implication of this finding is that   there was less   influence of learners= computer  literacy on   utilisation of online electronic books among the distance learning standards at the University of Nairobi. great they"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"furniture","label":"page_footer","orig":"113","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":50.33333333333337,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":314.3333333333333,"r":334.333333333333